@@ -0,0 +1,70 @@
+crew#684, and the founder's own analysis of it (docs/reference/incidents/2026-08-30-three-incidents-one-defect.md). Three incidents were one defect: an invariant that lived in a hand-kept list and was discoverable only in production. The list was `secret.reloader.stakater.com/reload: "a,b,c"`. It omitted the Langfuse entry in idp#955, it sat in namespaces the watcher never read in crew#684, and both times a green test asserted the list equalled the list. This deletes the surface rather than patching the lists.
+
+Optimised: 34 steps -> 11, 19 round trips -> 5; cut: the per-file edit-and-test loop over 13 manifests became one scripted sweep; four separate "does this workload reload" tests became one parameterised guard that discovers its own subjects from git, so a new workload needs no new test; the local run was cut to the 8 files that read the changed manifests (115 passed) because CI runs the full suite once on this PR (R58).
+
+## Why this is 41 files, over the 40 ceiling
+The change IS the sweep. One defect surface appears in 13 manifests and nine HelmRelease rows, and fixing them in 13 pull requests would leave the estate half-swept, where the new admission rule refuses manifests not yet swept. The base is the current origin/main (f3988c2f) and no other session's history rides along: one commit, and every file in it is either a hand-kept list deleted, an automatic uninstall taken back out, the rule that makes both inexpressible, or a test of those. Twenty-two of the 41 are two-line edits.
+
+## Options considered
+- Keep the per-workload lists and add a test that every mounted entry appears in its workload's list. Rejected: that is the control that already failed twice. It grades a list against a list, the same shape as the assertion that stayed green through both incidents — a test written from the same manifest it grades cannot see an entry nobody added to either side.
+- A CI-only check that greps the manifests. Rejected: it grades git, not the cluster, so a workload applied by any other path (a peer's overlay, a hand kubectl during break-glass) is unseen. The control has to sit where every workload converges, which is admission.
+- Chosen: rung 0 plus rung 1. Delete the list from every manifest (`reloader.stakater.com/auto: "true"`, which Reloader resolves against what the pod actually references, so there is no list to keep in step), and make the old shape inexpressible with a Kyverno policy that injects the annotation at admission and denies any workload carrying a hand-kept list.
+
+## Architecture laws
+- LAW 1 zero-gravity: `platform/edge/require-auto-reload.yaml` injects and refuses at admission; `platform/reloader/reloader.yaml` watches every namespace (`watchGlobally: true`), because an annotation in an unwatched namespace is inert and inert reads exactly like working
+- LAW 2 fractal: `python3 -m pytest -q tests/test_incident_crew684_every_workload_restarts_when_its_config_changes.py`
+- LAW 3 nervous system: `tests/test_incident_crew684_every_workload_restarts_when_its_config_changes.py` (6: the workload table is non-empty so an empty sweep is never green; every workload outside the system namespaces carries auto=true; no workload keeps a list of names; an opt-out is "false" plus a written reason; the watcher watches every namespace; the control is a mutation the cluster applies, not a line in a manifest; and the control is run against the pre-fix healthchecks Deployment on every gate run)
+- LAW 45 the mistake ends as a guard proved over every instance: five per-workload assertions deleted, one estate-wide guard added — net minus four controls, and the one guard discovers its subjects from git so a new workload needs no new test
+Cost-delta-usd-month: 0 (one more rule on an admission webhook that already runs; Reloader's watch widens from 6 namespaces to the cluster)
+Drill: cluster-state
+Tile: the estate state page — a workload that drops the annotation is refused at admission, so the red never reaches the founder's Ops view
+
+## Proof the control works, on the OLD broken files
+The founder's closing condition was: run the new rule against the pre-fix files and show it catching them. It is not a one-off demo — `tests/fixtures/reloader/hand-kept-list.bad.yaml` is the pre-fix healthchecks Deployment verbatim, and the guard runs the policy against it on every gate run, both ways.
+
+```
+$ kyverno apply platform/edge/require-auto-reload.yaml --resource tests/fixtures/reloader/hand-kept-list.bad.yaml
+policy require-auto-reload -> resource healthchecks/Deployment/healthchecks failed:
+1 - no-hand-kept-reload-list Deployment healthchecks names the Secrets or ConfigMaps it reloads on
+    by hand. That list is the bug surface: it went stale silently in idp#955 and crew#684, because a
+    list missing yesterday's entry looks like a complete one. Delete it.
+pass: 1, fail: 1, warn: 0, error: 0, skip: 0
+```
+
+The same file with the list replaced by `auto: "true"` gives `fail: 0`.
+
+Run against the tree, the estate-wide guard also refused a real manifest rather than a fixture:
+
+```
+FAILED ...[platform/hermes-agent/gateway.yaml::Deployment/hermes-agent-gateway]
+AssertionError: platform/hermes-agent/gateway.yaml Deployment/hermes-agent-gateway carries
+reloader.stakater.com/auto=None.
+```
+
+Being exact about what that proves: gateway.yaml is swept by the Otto branch as well (5a151c2b, PR 1014), and this branch is based on origin/main where that has not merged, so the guard was reading the live state of main — it is not a fourth list nobody had found. What it does show is the guard grading real manifests it discovered itself, not only the fixture its author wrote. The edit here is byte for byte the one PR 1014 makes, so whichever merges first the other is a no-op. Both branches also fix the crew#516 CP4 assertion that read the deleted list and had been raising KeyError since 5a151c2b (CI run 33351298943 on PR 1014).
+
+## Automatic uninstall is taken back out
+idp#1027 set `remediation: { strategy: uninstall }` on nine HelmRelease rows. Founder, 2026-08-30: uninstalling a wedged release turns a degraded service into a deleted one — its PVCs, PDBs and mounted state go with it — and it erases what says which invariant was violated, which is the state an incident is converted into a control from. All nine are back to a bounded rollback; repaving stays a hand after capture (`bin/idp-oke-break-glass helm-retry`).
+
+`platform/spire/helmrelease.yaml` is the one row that keeps install remediation, and it now says in git why. It was not set by idp#1027 — bc6dcfef (#312, 2026-08-27) set it for the opposite failure, a release stuck in Failed whose stale DaemonSet went on creating refused pods for 24 minutes — and SPIRE holds no PVC or PDB of its own to lose, since SVIDs are re-issued on restart.
+
+## Verify
+Verify: `python3 -m pytest -q tests/test_incident_crew684_every_workload_restarts_when_its_config_changes.py`
+Verify: `python3 -m pytest -q tests/test_spire_row.py tests/test_incident_crew516_cp4_gateway_probe_reaches_a2a_and_hermes_reads_the_allowlist.py`
+
+The first of those is the founder's proof obligation running on the runner, not on my laptop: it includes `test_the_control_refuses_the_state_it_was_written_for`, which runs the policy against the pre-fix healthchecks Deployment and fails if the policy admits it. That test skips as BLIND when the Kyverno CLI is absent, and the verify job did not have it — a job that exits 0 having proved nothing is the silent-green class this estate has hit four times — so this PR installs the CLI in `.github/workflows/verify-claims.yml`, pinned to the same v1.19.0 ci.yml uses. Any future `Verify:` line that claims something about admission can now actually prove it.
+
+## Definition of done
+1. Tracked item — crew#684, founder handover 2026-08-31 (~/.claude/docs/founder/2026-08-31T0002Z-handover-everything-this-session-was-carrying-2026-08-efb5c201.md)
+2. Code or config — 13 manifests swept, `platform/edge/require-auto-reload.yaml`, `platform/reloader/reloader.yaml`, nine HelmRelease rows
+3. Gate proved both ways — the block above: pre-fix file `fail: 1`, fixed file `fail: 0`, run on every gate and not once by its author
+4. Reference doc — `docs/reference/incidents/2026-08-30-three-incidents-one-defect.md`
+5. How-to and demo — a workload with a hand-kept list is refused by `kubectl apply`; one with no annotation is admitted with `auto: "true"` already on it
+6. Catalog entity — existing idp platform components; no new entity
+7. Operational proof — after merge, the cluster-state drill sees every workload annotated; the admission webhook already runs
+8. Scheduled re-grade — the guard on every gate run; cluster-state every 15 minutes
+9. Standard row — Secrets (a rotation reaching the running pod)
+10. Evidence block — below, attached by pr-evidence
+Standard: Secrets
+
+Author-session: 4a84e2ea
